t was all  right except I was homesick I really  loved the ranch and love being with my  parents and I didn't wanna be away so I  remember those years with considerable  pain and actually what was it like when  you found out that your mother had been  married before well I was very shocked  what year was that I don't remember I  was a student in El Paso and I remember  one of the children I knew saying I know  something about you know you don't yes I  do I know something about you and your  mother no II don't  what is it well your mother was married  before she had another husband and I  said oh that's not true I know you're  wrong and when I went home to my  grandmother's that evening I asked her  about it I said now somebody at school  told me that mo had been married before  that's not true  and my grandmother said well actually it  is she she had a brief marriage before  she went and married your father and I  guess that was something that in those  days  vorse wasn't encouraged and probably  looked down upon a little bit I don't  know anyway it had never been mentioned  to me and I was very shocked you talked  to your mama finally I did and she  couldn't believe that I had heard that  but she said well yes it's true now  that's that let's go on the picture in  the book of you when you were 16 years  old if I read it right you went to  Stanford that year I did yes and  graduated from law school by the time  they were what 20 I was Norm's close to  22 when I wanted to well I want to take  you to get out of Stanford well I  finished my major in three years but I  needed some additional credits to  account for the undergraduate degree and  I applied to the law school for early  admission at the law school and to my  great surprise they took me so my first  year of law school counted as credits  for my undergraduate degree and so then  I had two additional years of Law School  what were you like when you were 16  ignorant naive about what well about  what life for a