ral days together about  three years ago in the summer up in a  little cabin we have out of Prescott  Arizona  and we just talked about the things that  we thought ought to be included and we  agreed that it needed to tell the  history of the ranch it needed to  describe it in ways that people could  experience it and we needed to talk  about her parents and the Cowboys and  the cattle and the horses and what goes  on but it's like what's ranch life all  about and probably end up trying to  describe what we think the future of  public lands ought to be and how it  ought to be  how did you get Random House to  by the book well I didn't we didn't have  an agent and I had talked to Jim and  Kate Lehrer Jim writes books all the  time and I asked them one time how in  the world can you be so busy and write  those books one right after another and  he said well I try to write at least two  pages every day of my life  and I said how can you do that he said  both I'm on an airplane I do it on the  airplane if I'm at home I do it there if  I'm at my office I do it there and he  said you do that anybody can squeeze out  time to do that and I took that to heart  and then I talked to Kate Kate has also  published two or three books and I said  well Kate Huth you used as an editor who  what do you do you recommend somebody  and she said yes there's this woman at  Random House that I think you would like  why'd you call her and I did and it was  Kate Medina and she came to visit me and  we talked about the possibilities and  frankly I wasn't sure that it would work  I wasn't sure that what emerged would be  of sufficient quality that I would be  proud of it I just didn't know what I  was getting into and Kate Medina was  very kind and very patient and I didn't  want an advance because if I didn't like  it I didn't want anything to do with it  I wanted to see it before I knew that I  wanted to go forward with this I mean if  you put your name on something you want  to think well n